## 2.6  Characterization
The microscopic and structure characterizations of aerogel samples were carried out through scanning electron
To measure the MA performance, MOF/rGO-derived magnetic-dielectric aerogels were impregnated with paraffin. Then the obtained composites were cut into concentric rings with an inner diameter of $3.04\mathrm{mm}$ and outer diameter of $7.00~\mathrm{{mm}}$ . Electromagnetic parameters were measured using a coaxial method on a vector network analyzer (Agilent 5324A) in the frequency range of $2-18\mathrm{GHz}$ . The filling loading content of as-prepared aerogels in paraffin wax matrix can be calculated as follows:
$$
w=\rho_{\mathrm{aerogel}}\times\nu_{\mathrm{ring}}/m_{\mathrm{ring}}
$$
where $\rho_{\mathrm{aerogel}}$ is the density of aerogel, $\nu_{\mathrm{ring}}$ and $m_{\mathrm{ring}}$ is the volume and the weight of the ring, respectively.
## 2.8  RCS Simulation
COMSOL Multiphysics 5.6 was used for simulating the radar cross-sectional of the MOF/rGO-derived magnetic-dielectric aerogels. According to metal back model, the simulation model of the specimens was established as a square $(10\times10\mathrm{cm}^{2},$ ) with dual layers, which consisted of an aerogel absorption layer and a back plate of the perfect electric conductor (PEC). The thickness of the bottom PEC layer was $1.0\mathrm{mm}$ , and the absorber layer thickness values were set as 2.5 and $3.5\mathrm{mm}$ at the frequency of 15 and $10~\mathrm{GHz}$ , respectively. The aerogel-PEC model plate is placed on the X-O-Y plane, and linear polarized plane electromagnetic waves incident from the positive direction of the $Z$ axis to the negative direction of the $Z$ -axis. Meanwhile, the direction of electric polarization propagation is along the $X$ -axis. Open boundary conditions are setting in all directions with field monitor frequency of 10 and $15\mathrm{GHz}$ . The RCS values can be described as follows:
$$
\sigma(d B{\mathrm{~sm}})=10\log{\Big(}{\big(}4\pi S{\big/}\lambda^{2}{\big)}{\Big|}E_{s}{\Big/}E_{i}{\Big|}{\Big)}^{2}
$$
Here, $S$ is the area of the target object simulation model, $\lambda$ is the wavelength of electromagnetic wave, $E_{s}$ and $E_{i}$
represent the electric field intensity of scattered wave and the incident wave, respectively.
## 3  Results and Discussion
## 3.1  Synthesis of MOF/rGO‑derived Aerogels and Their Gelation Mechanism
$\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ and Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ magnetic and dielectric aerogels were prepared using a three-step procedure involving the synthesis of MOFs, MOF/rGO aerogels, and MOF/rGO-derived magnetic and dielectric aerogels. To fabricate MIL-88A nanorods, $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was first mixed with fumaric acid (organic ligand) in an aqueous solution. The resulting mixture was then subjected to hydrothermal treatment at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . By changing the composition of the metal ions (e.g., Ni: Fe ratio of 1:1 in the reactants), Nidoped MIL-88A nanorods were obtained. The morphology of the as-prepared MIL-88A and Ni-doped MIL-88A nanorods was characterized using SEM. The SEM images of both the MIL-88A and Ni-doped MIL-88A nanorods reveal highly uniform hexagonal rod-like nanostructures (Figs. S1a, b and S2a, b). Based on the elemental mapping images of MIL-88A, Fe, C, and O are homogeneously distributed in the hexagonal nanorods (Fig. S1c–e). An additional elemental Ni is also detected in the Ni-doped MIL-88A sample (Fig. S2c–f). After introducing Ni, the average length and width of the hexagonal nanorods decrease from 3.3 to $1.9\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and 650 to $550\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Fig. S3), indicating a change in the growth rate and crystal size. Furthermore, the doping process did not considerably modify the crystal structure and composition of MIL-88A [27], which was confirmed from its XRD patterns (Fig. S4).
Figure 1 schematically presents the preparation processes of 3D MOFs/rGO aerogels and their derived aerogels. In brief, a GO aqueous suspension and a presynthesized MOF crystal suspension (e.g., containing MIL-88A) were mixed under vigorous shaking conditions. Owing to the metal–oxygen covalent or electrostatic interactions between the free $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ on the MOF crystal surfaces and the oxygenated functional groups (i.e., $-\mathrm{OH}$ and –COOH) of GO, the precipitation of insoluble MIL-88A nanorods could be prevented in the GO nanosheets, thus affording stable suspensions. The mixed suspensions were rapidly transformed into wet gels in $10\mathrm{min}$ under moderate heating conditions. Under prolonged gelation time, the color of the gels gradually changed from brown to black, accompanied by volume shrinkage (Fig. 2a). This suggests that GO can be sufficiently reduced using MIL-88A without the use of additional chemicals or reagents.
To further explore the MOF-mediated gelation process, the evolution of the crystal structure and morphology of MIL-88A/rGO aerogels prepared at different gelation times was investigated (Fig. 2a). In the XRD patterns (Fig. 2b), the peak intensity gradually decreases and only two subpeaks of the (101) and weak (200) crystallographic facets are retained. The MIL-88A nanorods were randomly coupled with rGO nanosheets (Fig. 2c–g). The average length of the nanorods decreased from 2.5 to $1.6\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 2c2–g2). Moreover, the shape of the nanorod ends transformed from a hexagonal cone to a dome. Subsequently, nanorods with shortened ends were formed, finally producing small nanorods with dome-like shapes (Fig. 2c1–g1). During the gelation process, the MIL-88A nanorods were though to decompose and coordinate with the oxygenated groups of GO at both ends of the hexagonal structures, producing small nanorods anchored on the GO sheets and subsequently affording stable gels.
Therefore, the MIL-88A-mediated gelation of GO is divided into two steps. In the first step, the electrostatic interaction between free $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and the functional groups on a GO surface destroy the electrostatic repulsive forces between the GO nanosheets. In the second step, the crosslinking of GO nanosheets is assisted by $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ functioning as linkers, promoting the stacking of GO nanosheets and initiating the assembly of the sheets into a 3D network. Such a gelation process is easy to achieve and does not involve any complicated synthetic step and the use of additional reagents. Furthermore, aerogels with different weight ratios of MOFs and GO were prepared by adjusting the initial concentrations of MOFs in mixed suspensions. MIL-88A/rGO and Ni-doped MIL-88A/rGO aerogels with different MOFs/GO weight ratios were obtained (Figs. S5, S6), which show uniform cylindrical structures from top to bottom, indicating the homogeneous distribution of MOFs in the aerogels without precipitate formation. At a low weight ratio of MOFs and GO of 1:10, a low-shrinkage aerogel was achieved owing to insufficient crosslinkers (i.e., $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ) in the reaction mixture.
Under an additional thermal treatment, the MIL-88A/rGO and Ni-doped MIL-88A/rGO aerogels were converted to $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ and Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ magnetic and dielectric aerogels, respectively, showing density values of 6.2 and $5.3\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel is ultralight and exhibits magnetic properties (Fig. 3a, b). Furthermore, it presents highly porous 3D structures with microscale pores, which are obtained via the interlinking of the rGO sheets (Fig. 3c). Pea-like core–shell nanocapsules, which were derived from the MIL-88A nanorods, are uniformly coated on the surface of the rGO sheets (Fig. 3d). These nanocapsules comprise a carbon shell and large $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{L-Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ cores (Fig. 3e–h). Similar pea-like core–shell nanostructures were observed in the TEM images of samples containing rGO nanosheets (Fig. 3i–k). The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) of the ${\mathrm{L}}{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanoparticles reveals lattice distances of 0.147, 0.242, and $0.257{\mathrm{~nm}}$ (Fig. 3l–m), corresponding to the (440), (222), and (311) planes of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Fig. 3n) also confirms the transformation of MIL-88A to $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Furthermore, the HRTEM (Fig. 3o) and corresponding FFT (Fig. 3p) patterns show $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles with sizes of $<50~\mathrm{nm}$ anchored on the rGO surface (Fig. 3j–k), suggesting that the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ coordinated with the oxygenated groups of rGO was converted to $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ during high-temperature annealing.
In the case of the Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel, the inner morphology also shows a 3D interconnected porous microstructure (Fig. 4a) with numerous cocoonlike core–shell nanocapsules on rGO walls (Fig. 4b). The elemental mapping images of the Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel (Fig. 4c) show the distribution of Fe, O, C, and Ni in the rGO sheets. The enlarged SEM and TEM images further confirm the presence of cocoon-like nanocapsules, which consist of a thin shell and nanoparticle cores (Fig. 4d–f). The magnified HRTEM image (Fig. $\mathrm{4g}$ ) reveals lattice fringes with a spacing distance of $0.295\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (220) plane of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Clear diffraction rings are observed in the corresponding SAED pattern (Fig. 4h), where the components are consistent with those observed in the XRD results (Fig. 5a).
The structures and composition of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ and Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}}_{4}@{\ensuremath{\mathrm{C}}}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogels were further examined using XRD and XPS. The XRD patterns of the aerogels exhibit the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and a broad peak at $2\theta=26^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (002) reflection of rGO sheets (Fig. 5a). However, a tiny shift in the diffraction peaks of the Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel is observed (Fig. 5b). Based on the Bragg laws, an increase in $2\theta$ corresponded to a decrease in the d-spacing, which indicates a reduction in the magnitude of the lattice parameters. During pyrolysis, $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ would enter the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ lattice and replace $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , decreasing the lattice parameter because the ionic radius of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ $(0.069\mathrm{nm})$ is smaller than that of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\left(0.074\mathrm{nm}\right)$ [28, 29]. This phenomenon is also observed in the case of pure Nidoped MIL-88A nanorod–derived nanomaterials (Fig. S7).
The high-resolution $\mathrm{~C~}1s$ spectra of the aerogels (Fig. 5c, e) reveal binding energies of 284.6, 285.8, 288.2, and $289.8\mathrm{eV},$ , representing the C–C, C–O, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}=0$ and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, respectively, thus indicating a sufficient reduction of GO [30, 31]. The high-resolution Fe $2p$ spectra of the aerogels (Fig. 5d, f) show two obvious peaks at binding energies of 711.2 and $724.8\ \mathrm{eV};$ representing Fe $2p_{3/2}$ and Fe
$2p_{1/2}$ , respectively, thereby indicating the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [32, 33]. Ni is not detected in the XPS pattern of the Nidoped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel, ascribed to its trace amount. The ICP measurements reveals Ni (trace amount: 0.02 $\mathbf{wt}\%$ ) in the pure Ni-doped MIL-88A–derived $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ nanomaterial.
## 3.2  MA Performance
Aerogel/paraffin composites were fabricated by immersing the as-prepared aerogels in molten paraffin to retain the 3D porous structure (Fig. S8), instead of mixing rGO aerogel powders with paraffin to avoid the possible agglomeration and uncontrollable distribution of aerogels. The reflection loss (RL) curves and the two-dimensional (2D) and 3D representations of the RL for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ and Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogels (Fig. 6a–c) are calculated based on the transmission line theory using Eqs. 3 and 4 [34]:
$$
\mathrm{RL}=20\log{\left|\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_{0}}{Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_{0}}\right|}.
$$
$$
Z_{\mathrm{in}}=Z_{0}{\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{r}}{\varepsilon_{r}}}}\operatorname{tanh}\left(j{\frac{2\pi f d{\sqrt{\mu_{r}\varepsilon_{r}}}}{c}}\right)
$$
where $Z_{\mathrm{in}},Z_{0}$ , c, and f denote the normalized input impedance of the absorber, impedance of air, velocity of light, and frequency of microwaves, respectively. Furthermore, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}},$ $\upmu_{\mathrm{r}},$ and d represent the relative complex permittivity, relative complex permeability, and thickness of the absorber, respectively.
14 GHz at a thickness of 2.85 mm. Alternatively, the ­RLmin of the Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel exceeds $-20\mathrm{dB}$ at thicknesses of $2.5{-}5\mathrm{mm}$ (Fig. 6f) and the EAB range from 10.08 to $18~\mathrm{GHz}$ , with the maximum reaching $7.92\:\mathrm{GHz}$ , covering the entire $\mathtt{K u}$ band and $48\%$ of the $X$ band $(8{\mathrm{-}}12\ \mathrm{GHz})$ at a thickness of $2.8~\mathrm{mm}$ . The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ and Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathbf{O}}_{4}@{\mathbf{C}}/\mathrm{r}{\mathbf{G}}{\mathbf{O}}$ aerogels show efficient electromagnetic wave absorption in the entire $X$ band and $75\%$ of the C band at thicknesses of 3.5 and $5\mathrm{mm}$ , respectively (Fig. 6d). These results confirm the considerable effect of the size and composition of the original MIL-88A nanorods on the EM response capability, thus affecting the resulting MA performance.
To clarify the MA mechanism, the electromagnetic parameters including the real ( $\overline{{\varepsilon^{\prime}}}$ and $\mu^{\prime}$ ) and imaginary parts ( $\{\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ ) are shown in Figs. 7a and S11. $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\upmu^{\prime}$ represent the storage capability of electric and magnetic energies, respectively, and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ denote the dissipation capability of electric and magnetic energies, respectively. Generally, the dielectric loss of graphene-based aerogels is attributed to polarization relaxation and conduction losses ${(\varepsilon_{p}}^{\prime\prime}$ and $\mathrm{i}^{\prime\prime}$ , respectively), which are further confirmed using the Cole–Cole curves of $\varepsilon^{\prime}{-}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ according to the Debye relaxation theory [40, 41]. The curves are divided into two parts: the part with several semicircles and a small $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ value is related to the polarization loss and that with a straight line and a large $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ value is related to the conduction loss (Fig. 7c). The conduction loss is ascribed to the migration of electrons in the 3D interconnected network structure [2, 40] and the carbon shells of the nanocapsules [34, 42, 43]. The multiple polarization relaxations in the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{r}\mathrm{GO}$ and Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogels include the dipolar polarizations caused by the defects and functional groups on the rGO skeleton and multiple heterogeneous interfacial polarizations among $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ or Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ nanocapsules, small ferromagnetic nanoparticles, and rGO sheets [44, 45]. Moreover, based on doping engineering, the crystal structure of Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ can be modified and additional heterogeneous interfaces can be formed [46]. Compared with the pea-like $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ nanocapsules, the cocoon-like Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ nanocapsules shows highly maximized interfacial areas, reinforcing interfacial effects.
Because of the uniform incorporation of a ferromagnetic component from MIL-88A to the dielectric rGO aerogels, magnetic loss is also instrumental in the microwave attenuation capacity of both the aerogels. The $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ values of both the aerogels stable in the range of $0.91\mathrm{-}1.12$ and fluctuate in the range of $-0.05\substack{-0.15}$ (Fig. 7a). The negative $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ value indicates that the magnetic energy from the induced magnetic field of the materials is transformed into the electric energy. Similar results have been observed in other magnetic carbon absorbers [47]. Generally, the magnetic loss is attributed to the eddy current and magnetic resonance loss (natural and exchange resonances) [3]. The eddy current loss is evaluated using Eqs. 5 and 6 [11].
$$
\mu^{\prime\prime}\approx\frac{2}{3}\pi\mu_{0}\mu^{\prime2}\sigma d^{2}f
$$
$$
C_{0}=\mu^{\prime\prime}\mu^{\prime-2}f^{-1}=\frac23\pi\mu_{0}\sigma d^{2}
$$
where $\upmu_{0}$ denotes the vacuum permeability. $C_{0}$ is positively correlated with $d^{2}$ and $\sigma$ (conductivity). The $C_{0}$ value is almost stable in the range of $14{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ in the $C_{0}{-}f$ curves (Fig. 7d), indicating a magnetic loss from the eddy current loss. Alternatively, the large vibration area (blue dotted frame) of $C_{0}$ at low frequencies of $2{\mathrm{-}}7~\mathrm{GHz}$ and low fluctuation area (red dotted frame) at high frequencies of $8{\mathrm{-}}14~\mathrm{GHz}$ are dominated by natural and exchange resonances, respectively [3]. Furthermore, the increased weight ratio of MOFs and rGO (3:2) induces the agglomeration of MOF derivatives (Fig. 12), affording reduced magnetic loss (Fig. S11c, d).
In addition to the intrinsic microwave attenuation capacity (Fig.  7e), impedance matching is another factor for evaluating the prepared high-efficiency absorbers [11, 12]. Impedance matching is used to characterize the degree of matching between the input impedance of the material and the impedance of a free space. If the impedance matching value $(Z{=}|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}|)$ is $\sim1$ , additional electromagnetic waves enter the absorbers; otherwise, electromagnetic waves are reflected from the material surface. The impedance matching values in a moderate region of 0.8–1.2 for both aerogels increased (Fig. 7f, g), affording improved electromagnetic wave loss performance.
The relation between the $R L$ , thickness, and frequency can be obtained using the one-quarter wavelength model. The simulated thickness $(t_{m})$ of an absorber at $2{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ is estimated using Eq. 7 [15]:
$$
t_{m}={\frac{\mathrm{nc}}{4f_{m}{\sqrt{\left|\mu_{r}\right|\left|\varepsilon_{r}\right|}}}}
$$
where $n$ and $f_{\mathrm{m}}$ denote the refractive index of the material and the matched frequency, respectively. When the $t_{\mathrm{m}}$ value is approximately consistent with the experimental thickness of a material, the resulting MA shows practical applications. Figure $\mathrm{7h}$ , i show the typical $\mathrm{RL}{-}f,t_{\mathrm{m}}{-}f,$ and $\mathsf{I Z}_{\mathrm{in}}/\mathsf{Z}_{0}\mathsf{I}{-}f$ curves of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ and Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogels at thicknesses of 2.50 and 2.85 mm, respectively. The ­RLmin values are obtained at a frequency where $\lvert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\rvert=1$ and the $t_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{~\tiny~exp~}}$ values exactly falls in the $\lambda/4$ curves.
Figure  8 presents the CST simulation results for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathbf{O}}_{4}@{\mathbf{C}}/\mathrm{rGO}$ and Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}}_{4}@{\ensuremath{\mathrm{C}}}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogels at 10 and $15~\mathrm{GHz}$ , which reflect the real far-field conditions of MA performance [48]. At $10\mathrm{GHz}$ , both the aerogels exhibit weak scattering signals (Fig. 8a, b). The RCS value of the Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{r}\mathrm{GO}$ aerogel with a coating thickness of $3.5~\mathrm{mm}$ is less than − 10 dB sm in the range of $-72^{\circ}<\theta<72^{\circ}$ , which is lower than that of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}/$ rGO aerogel (Fig. 8c). The difference in the RCS values of the two aerogels is 15.1 dB sm at $\theta=0^{\circ}$ . At $15\ \mathrm{GHz}$ , the Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel shows a stronger scattering signal than the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel (Fig. 8d, e). The RCS value of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel is less than − 10 dB sm over the range of − $37^{\circ}<$ theta $<37^{\circ}$ at a coating thickness of $2.5\mathrm{mm}$ . These simulated results further confirm the enhanced MA performance of the Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel in the $X$ band. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel is superior to the Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}}_{4}@{\ensuremath{\mathrm{C}}}/\mathrm{rGO}$ aerogel in terms of scattering signal at high frequencies, corresponding well with the MA properties of the former summarized in Fig. 6f.
## 3.3  MA Mechanism
Based on the aforementioned experiments and simulation analysis, the MA mechanism of the MOF/rGO-derived magnetic and dielectric aerogels is attributed to the synergistic effects of impedance matching and microwave attenuation capability (Fig. 9). At the macroscopic level, a 3D porous structure promotes the entry of electromagnetic microwaves into the aerogels instead of being reflected from the surface. Multiple random reflections and scatterings of electromagnetic waves occur repeatedly in microcellular free spaces, affording excellent impedance matching [40]. At the microscopic level, the synergistic dielectric and magnetic losses are crucial in the absorption attenuation mechanism of the MOF/rGO-derived magnetic and dielectric aerogels. The incident electromagnetic microwaves are captured and attenuated by the 3D multicomponent walls composed of the rGO nanosheets, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ or Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ nanocapsules, and small ferromagnetic nanoparticles [18]. The multiple polarization relaxations in the aerogels include the dipolar polarizations caused by the defects and functional groups on the rGO skeleton and multiple heterogeneous interfacial polarizations of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ or Ni-doped $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ nanocapsules, small ferromagnetic nanoparticles, and graphene sheets [12]. Moreover, the interconnected and conductive structure of the aerogels effectively promote the conduction loss. Furthermore, spatially dispersed ferromagnetic nanoparticles suspended within highly porous 3D frameworks afforded a multiscale magnetic network and could considerably contribute to the enhanced magnetic responding capacity [19].
